## Finding-by-finding disposition

**P2, cross-body `:jump`/`:branch-false` not rejected: CLOSED.**
- `jump-scope-rule` at `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc:479` builds the pc-to-body map via `owner-of` and, for every `:jump` (target at operand index 1) and `:branch-false` (target at index 2), returns a `:jump-scope` tuple defect when the target's owner differs from the instruction's owner. Operand indices match `opcode-table`.
- Wiring in `body-rules` at line 601 is `[body-scope-rule jump-scope-rule register-bounds-rule terminator-rule live-shape-rule ...]`. It runs immediately after `body-scope-rule`, which is what makes `owner-of` total (bodies must contiguously cover every pc), and before `terminator-rule` and all four live-set rules, so `body-liveness` is never invoked on a cross-body image. This is the ordering the prior review asked for.
- The regression fixture is genuine. I evaluated it: the program lowers to body 0 at pcs 0-7 and body 1 at pcs 8-9. The test mutates the first `:jump`/`:branch-false`, which is the `:branch-false` at pc 2, retargeting 5 to 8. Pc 8 is in range (10 instructions), so `target-bounds-rule` passes; `body-scope-rule` only inspects `:closure` targets, so it passes too. The observed defect is exactly `{:rule :jump-scope, :pc 2}`, and the unmodified image still validates as nil. I additionally retargeted the `:jump` at pc 4 to 8 and got `{:rule :jump-scope, :pc 4}`.

**P3, unbacked `:move`/`:store-get`/`:store-put` absence claim: CLOSED.**
- `move-and-store-mnemonics-are-never-emitted` at `test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_compile_test.cljc:236` iterates every instruction of every image in `register-corpus` (the parity corpus minus deferred-node programs) plus the six B2 fixtures, asserting the mnemonic is not in the forbidden set. This is the full register-lowerable corpus, not a narrower sample.
- Corpus coverage is meaningful. Evaluated histogram over the 23 lowerable parity programs: branch-false, jump, closure, call, load-bound, load-free, const, return, halt all appear; none of the three forbidden mnemonics do. The `opcode-table` docstring's "asserted by this phase's own tests" claim is now true.

**P3, fixture E off-by-one comment: CLOSED.**
